<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jilin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jilin province</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jilin is a province of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>. Its lands were part of the ancient kingdoms of Buyeo, Goguryeo and Balhae, and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, which long restricted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> settlement there. From 1932 to 1945 it formed part of the Japanese puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jilin.jpg" alt="Jilin" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dcpeets · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Changbaishan_Tianchi_from_western_rim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
